  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124469,
      "id": "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379",
      "title": "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2691bbb3-d23b-5092-8566-26879620a6ca",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "If $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf{i}+e^{2 t}\\,\\mathbf{j},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(4 x + 2 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+4 x y\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=e^{2 t}$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(4(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+2(e^{2 t})^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+4(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(e^{2 t})\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2 e^{4 t} + 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i}+4 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{2 t})\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\mathbf{i}+2 e^{2 t}\\,\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2 e^{4 t} + 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i}+4 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\mathbf{i}+2 e^{2 t}\\,\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =((2 e^{4 t} + 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(4 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{2 t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{2 e^{4 t}}{t}+8 e^{4 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =8 e^{4 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{2 e^{4 t}}{t} + \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "adbcdb06-88b7-54c1-9795-67a564a7c241",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
